{
  "requirement": "`AgentConfig` and its backend-specific configuration types implement Serde\nserialization and deserialization. An application can embed them in its own\nconfiguration types; serializing and deserializing a supported configuration\npreserves its settings.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately cover `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-harness.agent_config.serialization`.\n\n**Obligations derived from the text**\n1. `AgentConfig` and its backend-specific types implement Serde serialization and deserialization.\n2. An application can embed them in its own configuration types.\n3. Serializing and then deserializing a supported configuration preserves its settings.\n\n**Coverage**\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/config.rs#mod[tests]/fn[all_backends_preserve_nested_settings_and_credentials]` is the main test. It covers all three backends (Bedrock, OpenAI, Ollama), and each case is a full configuration:\n  - Bedrock has `aws_profile`, `region`, and explicit `credentials` including `session_token`. It also has nested `options` with a map, a boolean, and an array of mixed string, integer and float values.\n  - OpenAI has `endpoint`, `api_key`, and a nested `reasoning` option.\n  - Ollama has `api_key_env` and a float option.\n- For each case the test:\n  - deserializes the JSON;\n  - asserts that `serde_json::to_value` equals the original value exactly, so no setting is dropped or altered;\n  - round-trips through a JSON string and asserts the resulting config equals the first parsed one.\n- That gives both directions of Serde and the preservation property. It also covers the backend-specific types (`BedrockConfig`, `OpenAiConfig`, `OllamaConfig`, `BedrockCredentials`, `OptionValue`) through the enum.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/config.rs#mod[tests]/fn[configuration_debug_hides_credentials]` adds three checks:\n  - `SecretString` serializes to its real value, so a credential is preserved on serialization even though its `Debug` output is redacted.\n  - An unknown field is rejected on deserialization.\n  - `SecretString` has no deserialize assertion of its own; the round trip in the first test covers it.\n\n**Embedding**\nNo test wraps these types in an application struct. Embedding follows from the types implementing `Serialize` and `Deserialize`. The tests call these through `serde_json` on the types themselves, so a missing implementation would fail them. I don't count the absence of a wrapper-struct test as a gap, because no distinct behavior or condition is involved.\n\n**Result**\nI found no plausible requirement-violating behavior that these tests would accept. The type definitions use `skip_serializing_if` and `default` for optional fields, and the tests exercise both the present and absent cases.",
